{
	title: 'hal/intro: a friendly first-run guide',
	spec: `hal/intro (src/host/intro.ts, registered in src/host/synthetic.ts; USER.md through src/host/profile.ts) is the first-run guide: a synthetic model that asks with forms and acts on the answers, with no provider needed. It is short because the user is eager to start. Every run asks the same questions; rerunning it updates the profile instead of skipping known fields. The flow, in order:

1) Greeting, exactly: "Hello — I am HAL 9001, your personal agent harness. You can call me Hal." Then, as a separate paragraph after a short pause, the number of questions this run will ask, for example "I have three questions for you." Name, model and language are the questions; the Serper question adds one only when no Serper key exists. Login and timezone confirmation are not counted. Apart from this greeting and the Dave placeholder there are no HAL 9000 references.
2) "What should I call you? (Optional)" as a text field whose placeholder is Dave. The placeholder is never saved.
4) Timezone, never typed as an IANA identifier by the user. It uses the client-reported timezone (task wq). When the client and host zones are equal and not UTC-like, save it without asking. When they differ, or both are UTC-like, ask "It seems like you are in the <city> timezone (<zone>). Correct?" as Yes/No. No offers a choice of this device's zone, the host's zone, or Other. Other takes a city or zone name, matched case-insensitively against Intl.supportedValuesOf('timeZone'), including by the city part, with a friendly retry when nothing matches. With no client signal, offer the host zone for confirmation unless it is UTC-like; otherwise leave the timezone unchanged.
5) Right after the name and timezone write USER.md (task ky) and say: "I saved your answers to <absolute path of USER.md>. You can edit it to update your personal preferences." Saving comes before login so a failed or abandoned login cannot lose the answers.
6) Login, unless auth.ason holds an account. It explains that /login signs in with Claude or ChatGPT and that API keys work too, offers to start /login claude, /login chatgpt or /login opencode, or Skip. Starting one ends the intro turn paused (reason: waiting for /login) without saying anything, then runs the real command; when that /login succeeds in a paused hal/intro tab the intro continues by itself, and Enter continues it anyway (a failed or abandoned login moves on without a model).
7) Model: "Which model should Hal use?" as a choice labelled with human display names, never raw IDs. It offers the current targets of the claude and gpt aliases, resolved through the model registry rather than hard-coded, each only when its provider has a credential, plus Other; when neither alias has a usable target (say, only an OpenCode Go key) the step is skipped and the close points to /model. Choosing a model sets the default for new sessions and switches this tab to it when the intro closes (switching earlier would hand the remaining answers to the real model). Other explains that /model or Ctrl-M picks any model. With no credentials at all, skip this step.
8) Serper web search setup: say "I recommend using Serper for web search results (serper.dev) - free tier goes a long way.", with serper.dev linking to https://serper.dev/signup; ask Yes or Maybe later; Yes takes the key in a secret field and stores it through apiKeys.save; an empty key or Escape (a skip form) skips the step and the intro moves on, so it never traps the user; skipped when a key exists.
9) Last, 'Any language or tone preferences? (E.g. "US English; spaces around em dash; friendly but concise")', saved as Language preference (USER.md is written or updated then). It comes last because the scripted intro cannot switch language mid-way.
10) Close with short tips (Escape pauses, Alt-Enter queues, /help, /keys, the web client address) and which model this tab now uses. With no usable credential the close says no model can answer yet. Whenever auth.ason holds no account, even with environment keys, the close ends by pointing to /login for Claude, ChatGPT and OpenCode Go subscriptions or API keys.

Pacing: an intro reply types out word by word, a tenth of synthetic.pauseMs apart, and its paragraphs arrive a pause (a few hundred milliseconds) apart while the turn streams. Pauses never block typing, scrolling or answering, and the user is never left without a way to answer or type. If the user submits text while the intro has no pending question, reply: I see you typed: "<text>" — but I'm not a language model, so I can't understand you. Continue with the intro? (Yes/No). Yes continues (after a finished run it starts a new run with the greeting) and No ends the intro, keeping what was saved. Text sent while a question was open waits in the inbox and is let be, not answered with this. Every intro choice is a handful of options that fits one screen; letters in a choice only pick an option, so a long list (such as every known model) leaves the user unable to see the question or type, which is the freeze this rule prevents.

It is resumable: after a restart or a steered message it continues from the first step of the current run that has no answer record, never asking twice within a run. A fresh home with no sessions opens its first tab on hal/intro, and the intro starts by itself: the tab's first turn runs without a prompt, so the greeting and first question appear without the user typing. Replay drops assistant messages before the first user message, so a real model chosen later still gets a conversation that starts with the user. Tests cover the step order with skips, resume mid-run, no credentials offering /login, the model options following credentials and aliases, and the timezone decisions. The sign-in offer appears unless auth.ason holds an account, even when only environment keys exist, and names any keys found. Each intro run says once, in its first login message (sign-in offer or model step), that /login signs in with Claude, ChatGPT and OpenCode Go subscriptions. Every intro question is skippable: Escape records an empty answer and the intro moves on (a skipped timezone stays unset), never leaving a paused turn only an answer can continue. /intro (src/host/commands/intro.ts) switches the tab to hal/intro and starts a fresh run at once, unless a turn is running or blocked; its output "Starting the intro." marks the restart, after which the intro greets again. The closing tips mention /intro.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'hal/intro keeps no step counter: its own durable messages mark a run (the greeting starts one, "I saved your answers to" means USER.md was written this run, so later edits by the user win over replayed answers) and named answer records hold the rest. The timezone confirmation and zone choice carry the zone in their own question text and options, read back from history, so a reconnect with another client zone cannot change an answered step. The first tab alone starts on hal/intro; provider tests with preexisting homes must seed a provider session. Login launches the real slash command after the intro turn; Serper uses apiKeys.save/liveFile with 0600 and its secret answer never enters history.',
		'E2E provider tests must wait for the preexisting session’s first host socket and then the peer UI before sending commands; session count is nonzero before either process starts, so using it as readiness races and drops keystrokes.',
		'Keep model choices tied to the available credential provider IDs, not the configured default; an unconfigured default must not sneak into the options. If the login step is skipped with no credentials, go straight to search.',
		'Invariant: city lookup accepts Kolkata on macOS and Linux even when ICU lists the valid older IANA name Asia/Calcutta. Normalise that supported city name to Asia/Kolkata before matching; the existing timezone decision test covers the real regression.'
	],
	needs: ['n2', 'wq']
}
